        "id": 1964575
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-11T01:38:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The count of entries in each list is constantly changing during traffic; there are typically more free tx buffers during an echo test. Really, we only expect shrink to have a positive effect after an atypical period where there is a lot of traffic that allocates a lot of extra buffers. So it\u0027s possible that applying a simple ratio like this is the way to go.\n\nIs it possible to share all buffers with read/write access so we could have a single list of free buffers? This would make it more straightforward to shrink.\n\nI don\u0027t believe LRU matters here, as all buffers are in the same memory region, they are all equally usable, and the use algorithm takes from the head and adds to the tail (as the existing tx algorithm worked). But there is another comment that suggests a stack algorithm for efficient.is useful here as we could always shrink from the beginning of the list and use/ buffers from the end",
      "parentUuid": "0e47b81f_ce3359c1",
      "revId": "66003080804e02ce241c6d39789253bc89be1bd5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
